00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,040
Hey everyone, we are now in the second video of this indirect price discrimination look.

2
00:00:05,120 --> 00:00:10,840
And here we're going to be looking at how pricing menus can be used to reveal customers willingness to pay.

3
00:00:10,840 --> 00:00:21,720
As opposed to looking at offering different versions of a product or different products, we're going to be looking at how the consumer through pricing can allow us to indirectly price discriminate.

4
00:00:21,720 --> 00:00:23,280
So here we're going to let the consumer choose.

5
00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:26,160
But what happens when the product remains the same?

6
00:00:26,880 --> 00:00:33,640
In this case, what we want to do is look at the different pricing structures or create what we call a pricing menu.

7
00:00:33,640 --> 00:00:35,360
So the same product is there.

8
00:00:35,680 --> 00:00:44,320
But now we offer a pricing menu to these consumers and allow them to choose what they want to consume based off that pricing menu.

9
00:00:44,480 --> 00:00:54,200
So here we'll be looking at things like volume discounts, bundling and usage fees to help us indirectly price discriminate into these markets.

10
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:58,920
So let's go ahead and jump into our first one which was volume discounts.

11
00:00:59,640 --> 00:01:05,560
Now this one occurs whenever we have a lower unit price for buying more.

12
00:01:05,640 --> 00:01:11,800
So here, customers who buy more paid less per unit, but they still pay more in total.

13
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:18,560
So our high value, high volume buyers, they're going to pay a lot more for what they have, but they paid less per unit.

14
00:01:18,560 --> 00:01:33,600
So again, if you think about paper towels as a particular example, as you purchase more and more paper towels, you will notice that the per unit paper towel roll price is actually lower than if you just bought two rolls of paper towels.

15
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:44,240
If you compare buying 24 versus 2, the per unit cost there is a lot less than it is for just the two, but you overall pay more for it.

16
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:51,440
So that again allows the firm to extract more of that consumer surplus from from those types of consumers.

17
00:01:52,320 --> 00:02:01,040
Now a second way that companies can offer the same product and do it at different effective prices is by using what we call two part tariffs.

18
00:02:01,040 --> 00:02:09,920
Here you charge an entry fee or an access fee, and then you charge a per unit fee for something else that's included inside of that particular product.

19
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:16,080
So here we're going to see the low and high value users both get access to the product that you're offering.

20
00:02:16,520 --> 00:02:23,320
But then you find that the high value users end up paying more for any additional add ons that go along with the particular product that you're offering.

21
00:02:23,320 --> 00:02:44,040
So if you're the classic example of gym membership, where you pay $50 per month to get access to the gym and all the equipment, all that good stuff, but then there might be a variable cost that's imposed upon the person, the high value consumer, who wants to go to the Pilates classes, the yoga classes or whatever it is that goes along that's extra inside of that gym membership.

22
00:02:44,040 --> 00:02:46,600
So that is a way to look at a two part tariff.

23
00:02:46,600 --> 00:03:00,360
You could also do the same thing for sports tickets where you charge a consumer a, an access fee to have an access to the seat for the, for the season.

24
00:03:00,360 --> 00:03:08,080
And then you charge them the individual game prices or ticket prices for those individual games as the season goes on.

25
00:03:08,080 --> 00:03:15,320
So that's a way to capture more of that surplus from those consumers and through the two part tariff.

26
00:03:15,960 --> 00:03:27,400
Now bundling is another way to look at our how to extract more consumer surplus out of the market when we do not know how our consumers are segmented.

27
00:03:27,640 --> 00:03:32,520
Now, bundling occurs whenever a firm is selling two or more products together.

28
00:03:32,520 --> 00:03:36,320
As a patent, we also call this a package tie in sale.

29
00:03:36,320 --> 00:03:42,760
And another important thing about bundling here is that firms are going to be using it whenever consumers value goods differently.

30
00:03:42,760 --> 00:03:49,280
That is, you have two consumers looking at two at a good, but one consumer values it highly, one values it lowly.

31
00:03:49,280 --> 00:03:53,000
The valuations are uncorrelated or negatively correlated.

32
00:03:53,000 --> 00:04:01,440
And that simply means that you have two people who look at the same good, or excuse me, they look at two different goods that are going to be bundled together.

33
00:04:01,520 --> 00:04:05,440
One consumer values one good and has no value whatsoever in the other good.

34
00:04:05,680 --> 00:04:10,720
But the other consumer values the good that the prior consumer had no value on.

35
00:04:11,120 --> 00:04:16,280
But then they value, they don't value the other good that the prior consumer had anything on.

36
00:04:16,280 --> 00:04:19,280
I'm going to make that a little bit more explicit here as we move on.

37
00:04:19,920 --> 00:04:22,960
Now there are two types of bundling that we deal with.

38
00:04:23,120 --> 00:04:24,640
The first is pure bundling here.

39
00:04:24,640 --> 00:04:27,840
The only way to purchase the items is through the bundle.

40
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:30,240
So you cannot purchase these items separately.

41
00:04:30,240 --> 00:04:32,600
They have to come together in a bundle.

42
00:04:32,600 --> 00:04:39,360
A classic way to look at this is through the classic or traditional cable TV package where you buy a bunch of channels for one price.

43
00:04:39,680 --> 00:04:41,800
Now there's also some versioning that goes on.

44
00:04:41,800 --> 00:04:49,000
You can buy tiered subscriptions or tier packages, but you can't buy those channels separately.

45
00:04:49,160 --> 00:04:55,960
So I might want hbo, but I might not want espn, or I might want espn, but I might not want Discovery.  Well, guess what?

46
00:04:56,040 --> 00:04:59,480
I have to get them all together inside of that same package.

47
00:04:59,720 --> 00:05:04,120
Now this works best whenever the standalone value is unclear.

48
00:05:04,600 --> 00:05:14,640
And what we mean by that is that consumers aren't really sure how much it's worth to them on its own, that is how much each product is worth them on its own.

49
00:05:14,640 --> 00:05:22,360
So in this case it's actually really good for the company to pull them all together and then sell them as a bundle.

50
00:05:22,600 --> 00:05:25,720
Now our next type of bundling is mixed bundling.

51
00:05:25,720 --> 00:05:29,960
Here consumers can buy those items together or they can buy them separately.

52
00:05:30,280 --> 00:05:34,600
But we will find that consumers get a discount from purchasing them together.

53
00:05:34,920 --> 00:05:36,720
Classic example is fast food.

54
00:05:36,720 --> 00:05:40,000
You can purchase the burger, fries and soda or all separately.

55
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:47,200
Or you could buy the combo which is going to give you a discount compared to if you bought all those items separately.  Here.

56
00:05:47,280 --> 00:05:54,360
This is going to encourage those high value users to actually go out and get the bundle as opposed to getting them separately.

57
00:05:54,360 --> 00:06:00,880
Whereas the low value users, they might just buy one of the items separately and then they'll care for the rest of the items.

58
00:06:00,960 --> 00:06:04,880
Now why bundling works because it captures more consumer surplus.

59
00:06:05,480 --> 00:06:13,400
Before you might have a, if you had one price for a good and it was priced too high, you would lose a consumer inside of that market.

60
00:06:13,400 --> 00:06:15,480
So you lose that consumer surplus.

61
00:06:15,560 --> 00:06:27,640
But by combining items together and selling them for a lower price than what you otherwise would, you can attract more low value users into the purchase of that product, which leads into smoothing out those value gap.

62
00:06:27,640 --> 00:06:47,600
And then here all that simply means is that when you bring customers several items together inside of a bundle, you're going to get a consumer who might value one item in that bundle, but doesn't really care for other items inside that bundle, but because it comes at a price that is comfortable for them, they're gonna be wanting, they'll be willing to buy that particular item.

63
00:06:47,600 --> 00:06:50,640
So for example, let's say that you're buying Microsoft Office.

64
00:06:51,120 --> 00:06:56,480
You really don't care about PowerPoint, but maybe you really do like using Excel.

65
00:06:56,480 --> 00:07:06,360
So if it comes together as a bundle at a price that's agreeable to you, you're more willing to purchase that bundle than might add a standalone value with just Excel.

66
00:07:06,360 --> 00:07:08,040
And you definitely don't want PowerPoint.

67
00:07:08,040 --> 00:07:12,520
Another example here is think about Spotify and their audio books there.

68
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:17,720
So here, why would you pay $12 just for one of those audiobooks?

69
00:07:17,720 --> 00:07:25,080
But when you bring it together inside of a bundle now it becomes an add on inside of that premium and it looks much more enticing to you.

70
00:07:25,080 --> 00:07:37,840
And then finally consumers again are allowed to self select into the, the, the, the high value, low value that we want them to because we cannot segment the market because it's just too difficult for us.

