00:09:52,920 --> 00:09:58,200
And this occurs when a firm requires a customer to purchase a complimentary good repeatedly.

94
00:09:58,200 --> 00:10:00,960
Now, this is more specifically called a requirement tie in sale.

95
00:10:00,960 --> 00:10:04,440
We previously saw the package tie in sale which dealt with bundling.

96
00:10:04,440 --> 00:10:07,320
But now we're looking at these requirement tie ins.

97
00:10:07,400 --> 00:10:31,440
Now, with these types of sales, typically what we're going to be seeing is that the base product here is going to act more like fee to get to the second product that is this, this first product might be sold at cost or at near cost in order to get the second product, which is going to be the higher margin product to be bought repeatedly by the consumer.

98
00:10:31,440 --> 00:10:35,760
So this is going to allow the firm to price discriminate based on usage.

99
00:10:36,080 --> 00:10:43,800
So heavy users are going to be consuming more, they're going to have a higher willingness to pay for it and that therefore it's going to reveal a higher margin for that firm.

100
00:10:43,800 --> 00:10:52,840
Whereas the light users, they aren't going to buy as much of the product, but they, and they're gonna pay less overall, but they still are buying both of the products together.

101
00:10:54,120 --> 00:11:06,200
And again, this is another classic example of not having to have different versions of your product, but just having a different price menu here because you don't need to figure out what the customer type is.

102
00:11:06,440 --> 00:11:19,280
You just give them a price menu and then allow them to determine how much they're going to purchase based off of their willingness to pay and how much value they get from that particular product.

